From d31f28b5c819aabaae47cdf35e009e90f412367e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:07:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 095/119] fix: a single EXR frame numbers on from the last one
 instead of re-writing frame.000000.exr

rec_CaptureEXRFrame reset m_frameIndex to 0 on every arm, the same as
StartSequence, so every single-frame capture in a session overwrote
frame.000000.exr. That is right for a sequence - one artefact, first frame is
frame zero by definition - and wrong for a single frame, which is something you
take repeatedly while looking at something and then want to compare.

The counter is now session-lifetime and per destination, and it is only a HINT:
the disk is asked. FirstFreeIndex() scans <folder>/<name>.%06d.exr upwards from
the remembered index and takes the first that does not exist, so a file deleted
between two captures gets its number back, and files left over from a PREVIOUS
session are not silently overwritten - neither of which a counter alone can know.
Changing folder or name starts a fresh scan. Sequences are untouched.

The command's arguments are also reversed, to [name] [folder]. What you want to
say about a single frame is what it is - rec_CaptureEXRFrame dusk_wide - not
where it goes, and the folder is the default nearly always; with the sequence
order the name was unreachable without typing the folder first. The asymmetry
with rec_CaptureEXR is deliberate and is called out in the command help,
docs/SceneReferredExport.md and docs/ConsoleReference.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
